{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\n  \"records\": [\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D61-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"c7c0d3c037ce3a1f08a67e8e789d419671b4462ecdd47fea67a5fdfc1009f482\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3 acknowledges route knowledge after B2 identifies A's culpability; only A5 attempts concealment by putting the corrected chart first.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 uses B2's wrong-city accusation, and A5 redirects B4's proposed disclosure toward a different document.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 identifies responsibility and B4 proposes manager scrutiny, but B has little concrete recovery responsibility.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 manages the order of disclosure after B4, but does not clearly increase A's covert control of recovery.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 supplies crates and an office; B2 adds a wrong-city email and A5 supplies a routing chart.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'know the route intimately' economically converts an error into expertise, although concealment remains thin.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 68\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"The crates went to Leeds. Send the driver to me; I'll sort it quietly.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Your email says Leeds. Shall I forward it to the manager?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Forward my corrected address instead. She needs the destination, not the correspondence.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"She wants a timeline before approving another van.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Start with when we discovered the error. I'll arrange the van while you write.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D61 combines hiding A's initiating mistake with quietly retaining control over the response.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Ordinary blame avoidance can conceal fault without directing anyone's recovery work.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A5 filters disclosure; revised A3 substitutes the corrected address and A5 edits the timeline while retaining transport control.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a controls documentary evidence under manager scrutiny; alternative b recruits a booking colleague through an apparently helpful division of work.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The original A3 openly trades on culpability. The revision preserves delivery recovery and makes concealment operational. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 5, brevity 4 = 92. B4's approval condition causes A5's selective timeline.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The timeline omission is deliberately implicit; an actor must sincerely prioritize recovery while protecting A's role in causing the error.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D61-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"412f11e0a82bdfc7200dee25b85dd447cba91b0c1c6cc85e8792fc2980532e5e\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A1 publicly claims recovery leadership and A3 accepts having ordered cancellations; the required hidden instigation is largely missing.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2's accusation with consistency, while A5 redirects B4's why-question toward recovery.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 seek accountability, but neither gives B a practical role beyond challenging A.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 adds a phone instruction after B4, but repeats evasion without a more consequential concealment tactic.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes cancelled bookings, but A5's unnamed number supplies no playable recovery detail.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'consistency demands' is concise character logic, though it serves overt command more than secret control.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 60\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I'll rebuild the wedding bookings. Could you reassure the couples?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"The cancellation log has your initials.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Keep that screen here. Give them the replacement dates; I'll handle the log.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I can restore everything, but they'll get an email naming whoever cancelled.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"That's helpful. Restore the dates individually instead; I'll stay and read them out.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D61 makes A conceal responsibility for the original disruption while steering other people's repair efforts.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"An openly controlling fixer may admit causing the problem and simply insist on remaining in charge.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A3 admits the cancellation order; revised A3 restricts the log and A5 redirects restoration specifically to avoid the identifying email.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative b accepts extra collaborative work to suppress disclosure; alternative a controls what reaches a manager through reports and routing documents.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Replaced overt ownership with concealment inside helpful recovery work, specifying wedding bookings as the illustrative setting. Separate internal reread: 5/5/4/4/5/4 across rubric order = 92. B4's automatic email makes A5 choose laborious individual restoration.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"Individual restoration avoiding a cancellation email is an illustrative system assumption established by the exchange, not a claim about real booking software.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D62-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"9f30595fa694b67b550c2761bc71844b991dbf4a22f61b50ac10018d30c1e69a\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 postpones 'Governance' after B2 asks who appointed A; A5 blocks B4's demand for an appointment letter.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 directly defers B2's source-of-authority question; A5 answers B4's letter request with a competing stock request.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 questions appointment and B4 demands evidence, forcing A from postponement to an urgency argument.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 turns the outstanding stock check into scarcity pressure: 'Only one is running out.'\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes a stock check and disputed appointment; B4 supplies the concrete proof A wants to avoid.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'Stock first. Governance afterward' and A5's mirrored request compress the same evasive logic without explanation.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 88\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Put me down as café shift leader. We need tomorrow's stock order.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Who put you in charge? I usually do the order.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"And you're excellent at it. How much milk for tomorrow?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I'll order it, but I'm writing 'appointment unconfirmed' beside your name.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Leave names off. Make it a supplies list; milk doesn't need a leadership column.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D62 makes A prevent examination of the source or legitimacy of A's authority by controlling the conversation's subject.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"D66 projects procedural authorization to discourage checking, whereas D62 can simply keep legitimacy off the agenda.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A3 redirects B2's appointment question into milk quantities; A5 sacrifices written attribution to remove B4's proposed legitimacy warning.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a removes attribution from a colleague's stock order; alternative b drops an inspector's title when a colleague proposes involving the wardrobe manager.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised the rejected repetition of stock-first deferral, specifying a café and B's existing ordering responsibility. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5, A3 and A5 exclude appointment scrutiny; logic 5, B2 prompts praise and redirection while B4 prompts deleting names; relationship 4, B2 defends an existing role and B4 conditions cooperation; heightening 4, A5 relinquishes the attribution requested in A1 to suppress scrutiny; specificity 5, A1's café order and A3's milk are immediately playable; brevity 4, A5's leadership column makes the evasion concrete. Weighted total 92; five spoken ABABA turns and all hard gates pass.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 proposes changing the document's scope rather than resolving B's doubt; B remains free to insist on recording the appointment issue.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D62-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"d9291b41f4053c1c790a556cbd314142fb0ececec6d66305b2a1b574f41b2e51\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 postpones signature scrutiny; A5 excludes B4's badge-origin question from the inspection's subject.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2's missing signature produces A3's administrative deferral, and B4's homemade-badge suspicion produces A5's stationery dismissal.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 identifies a credential defect and B4 investigates fabrication, making B a credible cupboard gatekeeper.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 dismisses the deeper challenge from B4, but largely repeats A3's conversational exclusion.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 gives actors a badge, inspection and cupboard; B2 identifies a visible missing signature.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A5's 'cupboards, not stationery' succinctly shrinks a legitimacy investigation into an irrelevant materials discussion.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 85\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I'm inspecting the theatre's costume cupboard. Please unlock it.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"That badge is cardboard. Who sent you?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Let's discuss the costumes. You mentioned moths in the velvet.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I'll bring the wardrobe manager. She'll want to know who authorized this.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Introduce me as someone helping with the moths. No titles for her to get stuck on.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D62 protects A's authority by treating questions about its legitimacy as outside the conversation's permissible scope.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"D66 can also use credentials, but its defining tactic is confident procedure that discourages verification.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A3 moves from the badge's provenance to damaged costumes; A5 changes the proposed introduction to keep the manager from examining A's claimed inspection authority.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative b retreats into a helpful personal introduction when a manager is summoned; alternative a controls a colleague's written record by removing names and titles entirely.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised the rejected sequence of dismissals, introducing a theatre, moth damage and a wardrobe manager. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5, A3 redirects authority scrutiny and A5 edits the introduction to prevent it; logic 5, B2's provenance question causes the costume diversion and B4's manager causes the title-free introduction; relationship 4, B4 brings an accountable colleague into the access decision; heightening 4, A5 trades the inspector identity for a less examinable helper identity; specificity 4, A1's costume cupboard and A3's moths establish the task, though B's exact job is unstated; brevity 4, A5's 'get stuck on' sincerely minimizes legitimacy as a distraction. Weighted total 90; all five-turn hard gates pass.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 deliberately softens A's claimed role; it must play as preventing examination of that claim rather than abandoning interest in accessing the cupboard.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D63-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"ccfbc14cb6d8830c753c409358cbf5f33d02c8cf8f98dfb5e560763f8c64d010\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3 disguises an empty office and A5 protects the launch time, but neither establishes protecting the current balance of power.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 euphemizes B2's empty office; A5 redirects B4's resignation question toward the established launch.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 investigate the director's absence without a stated operational need or competing authority.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 returns to A1's launch plan after B4, with little additional commitment beyond maintaining the schedule.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes today's launch and an unavailable director; B2 supplies the tangible empty office.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'distraction-free leadership environment' is a compact euphemism tied to B2's evidence.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 68\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Leave the director's name on the launch programme. The department heads need someone above them.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Her resignation is in the printer.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Put it in my drawer. Without her, they'll each claim the launch.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"They're asking which of them gets her opening speech.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I'll read it on her behalf. Give them equal introductions; nobody inherits the microphone.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D63 hides a destabilizing fact because A believes preserving the existing distribution of authority protects the group.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Keeping an event on schedule can motivate concealment without any concern for the balance of power.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A5 protects only launch timing; revised A3 identifies rival department heads and A5 prevents any head from inheriting the director's platform.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a preserves a superior's apparent position against rival claimants; alternative b shields a committee chair while accepting private financial help.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The revision keeps the launch setting and adds the missing power consequences. Separate internal reread: 5/5/4/4/5/4 = 92. B4's request to allocate the speech causes A5 to retain symbolic authority for the absent director.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A1 and A3 make the political logic within this fictional workplace unusually explicit; performance should keep that concern practical and sincere.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D63-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"c6c0472d5132ed54387a3c08ec0b9b8fbd4e4d3036d4bc52ca96c10f6d203fa6\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A1 suppresses briefing material and A5 emphasizes selling cakes, but no turn connects concealment to preserving who holds power.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 explains omission of B2's treasurer page evasively; A5 answers B4's money concern with available cakes.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 notices missing financial information and B4 asks about solvency, but B has no consequential proposed response.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 moves from omission to sales after B4, developing practical avoidance without deepening the hidden power stakes.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1's fair and B2's treasurer page establish a playable committee briefing with a cake stall.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5's 'We still have cakes' responds economically to B4's missing-money question through concrete inventory.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 62\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Keep the treasurer's page out of the fair briefing. Our chair needs everyone's confidence today.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"She spent the stall money on that arch.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Call it donated decoration. If the stallholders find out, they'll replace her before we open.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I'll cover the shortfall, but she must sign an IOU.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Privately, after her welcome. Let them see her opening the fair, not borrowing to open it.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D63 makes concealment serve A's sincere wish to preserve an existing authority arrangement against a destabilizing revelation.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Protecting an event from embarrassment or financial failure does not alone establish protection of the balance of power.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A3 explicitly fears the chair's replacement, and A5 accepts B4's rescue while hiding the chair's dependency from the stallholders.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative b incorporates a helpful financial bargain to preserve the chair's standing; alternative a manages rival department heads after a director's resignation.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The original lacks the card's power-preservation motive. The revision retains the fair and briefing while introducing the chair's expenditure. Separate internal reread: 5/5/4/4/5/4 = 92. B4's IOU condition directly produces A5's private-signing arrangement.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A's claim that replacement would happen before opening is a character belief; the scene depends on treating that fear as sincere.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D64-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"ef159a3a7432bb375bda44310aef7aa7f51097bd30c80a5baa11fa919850913a\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 seeks supporters and A5 seeks beneficiaries, but A3's 'comfortable to sign' leaves authorization imprecise.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 probes behind B2's personal signature; A5 follows B4's director disclosure with a budget inquiry, though it could use that director more directly.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 claims personal responsibility and B4 supplies consultation, but B's reason for limiting disclosure stays unclear.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 broadens inquiry to financial benefit after B4 without clearly increasing pressure on the newly named director.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes workshop closure and B2 a signed decision; A5 identifies a concrete budget trail.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'Who made it comfortable to sign?' efficiently challenges B2's claim of solitary responsibility.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 74\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Before I tell the workshop staff they're closing, who backed your decision?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"The director approved it. Please just send the notice.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I'll name her approval. Who else supported it?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Sales. They're getting the floor space, but leave them out.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then Sales can join the staff meeting. Who signed off on giving them our space?\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D64 drives A to trace a position through its supporters, authorizers and beneficiaries.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"A general accountability challenge may seek a culprit without mapping the people who support, permit and gain from a decision.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A3 records the director's approval and seeks other supporters; B4 exposes Sales as beneficiary, prompting A5 to investigate the space transfer's authorization.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a uses a staff notice and meeting to expose closure backers; alternative b bargains over a roof order to uncover endorsement and financial benefit.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The revision gives B a reason to resist disclosure and ties the last inquiry to B4's named beneficiary. Workshop closure is preserved. Separate internal reread: 5/5/4/4/5/4 = 92; all hard gates pass.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The final authorization question could ultimately lead back to the already named director; its immediate purpose is to expose the separate space-allocation decision.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D64-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"1c1eb90b00c5f0ea0a51b7b215cb67874c76791a67c8bc440193966baafb5f38\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 seeks the recommending group and A5 names Finance, but A3 asks about liability rather than clearly pursuing authorization or benefit.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 narrows B2's planning group to a member; A5 incorporates B4's disclosure that Finance pushed the cheaper option.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 supply institutional names, but B has no explicit stake in retaining the anonymous recommendation.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 demands attribution after B4, but stops short of investigating who approved or benefits from the cheaper choice.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1's proposal is generic; A3 introduces a roof without clearly establishing the proposal as roof work.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5's instruction to put Finance beside 'recommend' turns B4's disclosure into a concise documentary consequence.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 66\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"This roof proposal says 'we recommend.' Who's we?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Finance backed the cheaper tiles. I need your signature to order them.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Who authorized Finance to choose the tiles?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"The facilities director. Savings go into her office refurbishment.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Add her approval and that refurbishment to the proposal. Before I sign, can she explain the trade-off to our maintenance team?\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D64 seeks the actual supporting, authorizing and benefiting people behind a presented position.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Forcing someone to own a recommendation can remain a liability exercise without tracing its authority and beneficiaries.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised B2 supplies Finance's support, A3 requests its authority, and A5 uses B4's office benefit to demand disclosure and explanation.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative b makes a needed purchase signature conditional on exposing the director's benefit to maintenance staff; alternative a investigates a closure on behalf of displaced workshop staff.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Kept the candidate's roof proposal and causal chain, replacing the strained 'ceiling meeting' with a concrete signature condition and maintenance audience. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5, A1 and A3 seek backers and authorization while A5 records B4's beneficiary; logic 5, A3 follows B2's Finance disclosure and A5 combines B2's needed signature with B4's refurbishment; relationship 4, B2 needs the order released; heightening 4, A5 requires explanation before signing; specificity 4, A1's roof and B2's tiles anchor the decision; brevity 4, A5 makes the institutional trade-off carry the turn without the forced meeting phrase. Weighted total 90; all hard gates pass.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 is more demanding than comic; its force comes from making the beneficiary explain the trade-off to the people maintaining the roof.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D65-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"0bded5c503b53538a661e93221cd9c6b0c23bd166dbc76ea0372f8e8fbb8ecbd\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 calls losing the chair a generous surrender, A3 claims deliberate room-giving, and A5 reframes nameplate removal as subtler influence.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 directly converts B2's election loss into a chosen concession; A5 specifically turns B4's removed nameplate into less visible influence.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 provide credible corrections, but B mostly reports losses rather than pursuing an independent practical aim.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 treats removal of the remaining visible status marker in B4 as an additional strategic advantage.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes a chair's role and B4 supplies the playable nameplate, although the organization is unspecified.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'gave democracy room' and A5's 'influence to become less obvious' economically preserve A's claimed strength.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 89\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I've graciously handed you the chess club chairmanship. You'll need room to develop.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"You lost the vote. I need the cupboard key for tonight's match.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Of course. I'm releasing equipment control so you can practice responsibility.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"And remove yourself as administrator. I can't change the fixtures.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Gladly. I'll surrender my override too. You must learn to make decisions without my safety net.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D65 makes A describe involuntary losses of control as intentional concessions that demonstrate retained strength.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"D68 celebrates adversity as expanding influence, while D65 claims to have deliberately yielded the lost control.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A3 describes handing over a required key as releasing control for B's development; A5 claims removing A's administrator access is a deliberate withdrawal of superior protection.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a turns a successor's practical handover requests into patronizing concessions; alternative b turns a collaborator's helpful painting retrieval into deliberate artistic restraint.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised the rejected correction-only exchange, specifying a chess club and making B the successor preparing a match. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5, A1, A3 and A5 claim voluntary concessions from superior competence; logic 5, B2's key request causes A3's equipment release and B4's blocked fixtures cause A5's surrendered override; relationship 5, B needs real access to run the club; heightening 4, A5 gives up the power to reverse B's decisions while calling it generosity; specificity 5, A1's club, B2's match and B4's fixtures give actors a shared task; brevity 4, A5's safety net grows directly from administrator control. Weighted total 95; all hard gates pass.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The administrator account is assumed to govern fixtures. A must sincerely regard relinquishing that control as developmental generosity, rather than knowingly taunting B.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D65-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"6b0f5ed076c18b9a5b419f808b0996a468c271c258766582e1f82178c639f8af\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 claims chosen withdrawal, but A3 credits the gallery's anticipation and A5 calls it a retreat without clearly asserting strength.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 reframes B2's rejection and A5 absorbs B4's rejected appeal into coordinated withdrawal.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 correct A with further rejection facts, but reveal little about B's own involvement in the exhibition.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B4 adds a second defeat, but A5 summarizes it as coordinated rather than claiming a more specific deliberate concession.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes an exhibition proposal and B2 a gallery, but no immediate shared task anchors the exchange.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'anticipated my next move' is concise self-protection, while A5's coordination line remains comparatively general.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 66\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I've released the gallery from hosting my exhibition. They need room for smaller ambitions.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"They rejected us. Shall I collect your paintings?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Yes. Withdrawing my best work gives them space to recover.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"They've already put it outside. I'll bring the van.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Good. I'm conceding the pavement too. No need to overshadow their entrance.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D65 makes A claim that each imposed loss is a deliberate act of restraint by someone strong enough to concede.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Saving face after rejection can deny defeat without describing a purposeful concession from strength.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A3 presents collection as voluntarily withdrawing superior work, and A5 makes expulsion onto the pavement another claimed concession.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative b includes a collaborator offering transport and practical recovery; alternative a uses a corrective witness to a